---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import IdealGasLaw from '@/components/tools/calc2/IdealGasLaw.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="ideal-gas-law-calculator">
  <IdealGasLaw client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the ideal gas law calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The ideal gas law links the pressure, volume, amount and temperature of a gas. Choose the value
      to find, enter the other three in any of the units, and the ideal gas law calculator converts them
      to SI units, solves the equation and converts the answer back.
    </p>
    <pre><code>P V = n R T          R = 8.314462618 J/(mol·K)
P = nRT ÷ V     V = nRT ÷ P     n = PV ÷ RT     T = PV ÷ nR
1 atm = 101,325 Pa   1 bar = 100,000 Pa   1 psi = 6,894.76 Pa   1 mmHg = 133.322 Pa
T(K) = T(°C) + 273.15</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      One mole of gas at 0 °C (273.15 K) and 1 atm (101,325 Pa) takes up
      V = 1 × 8.314462618 × 273.15 ÷ 101,325 = 0.022414 m³, which is 22.414 L. At 25 °C the same gas takes
      24.465 L.
    </p>
    <p>
      A 10 L tank holds air at 200 kPa and 27 °C (300.15 K). It contains
      n = 200,000 × 0.010 ÷ (8.314 × 300.15) = 0.80 mol of gas.
    </p>

    <h2>The simulation</h2>
    <p>
      The dots stand for molecules. More moles give more dots; a higher temperature makes them move
      faster, in proportion to the square root of the absolute temperature, as the average molecular
      speed does. The piston moves out as the volume grows. The pressure is the push of all the hits on
      the walls: fewer hits in a larger box, harder hits in a hotter gas.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Real gases depart from the law at high pressure and near their boiling point. Use the van der Waals equation there.</li>
      <li>Use the absolute pressure, not the gauge pressure a tyre gauge shows. Add the air pressure (about 101 kPa) to a gauge reading.</li>
      <li>The temperature must be above absolute zero, 0 K or −273.15 °C.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
